<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Simplifying and adding two unlike surds to form a single surd</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Simplify the following expression, giving your answer in the form \(k\sqrt{m}\), where \(m\) has no perfect-square factors. <i>(Note: Write <code>6*sqrt(10)</code> to represent \(6\sqrt{10}\).)</i></p>
<p style="text-align: center; font-size: 1.2em;">\[ \sqrt{{@x@}} + \sqrt{{@y@}} \]</p>
<p>Simplified form = [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p><b>Step 1: Simplify the first square root</b><br>
Find the largest perfect-square factor of \({@x@}\). Since \({@x@} = {@a2@} \times {@n@}\):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle \sqrt{{@x@}} = \sqrt{{@a2@} \times {@n@}} = \sqrt{{@a2@}} \times \sqrt{{@n@}} = {@a@}\sqrt{{@n@}}\)</p>

<p><b>Step 2: Simplify the second square root</b><br>
Find the largest perfect-square factor of \({@y@}\). Since \({@y@} = {@b2@} \times {@n@}\):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle \sqrt{{@y@}} = \sqrt{{@b2@} \times {@n@}} = \sqrt{{@b2@}} \times \sqrt{{@n@}} = {@b@}\sqrt{{@n@}}\)</p>

<p><b>Step 3: Combine the like surds</b><br>
Both terms now share the same irrational part \(\sqrt{{@n@}}\), so add the coefficients in front:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle {@a@}\sqrt{{@n@}} + {@b@}\sqrt{{@n@}} = ({@a@} + {@b@})\sqrt{{@n@}} = {@c@}\sqrt{{@n@}}\)</p>

<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\(\displaystyle \boxed{ \sqrt{{@x@}} + \sqrt{{@y@}} = {@ta1@} }\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
